Every cup of coffee has a long trail behind it. The drink started with a plant in the forests of Ethiopia, became a religious aid in Yemen, got banned (and unbanned) more than once, built one of the first public spaces where strangers sat down and argued about the news, helped start one of the world's biggest insurance markets, and then lost out to tea in Britain.

This is the overview. The map and timeline below show the whole story at a glance, where each era has its own guide. The early parts are short, because we honestly don't know much. The detail picks up as the records get better and the story gets closer to the coffee in your kitchen.

## How coffee spread

Coffee started in one corner of Ethiopia. It took about 400 years to reach most of the places that grow it today, carried by pilgrims, traders, smugglers and colonial empires.

